+++
id = "ch-linear-eq-practice"
kind = "exercises"
title = "Practice: linear equations in one variable"
standards = ["CCSS.MATH.8.EE.C.7"]
+++

Difficulty runs 1–3. Exercises marked with a star in the printed edition
are difficulty 3. Answers appear in the teacher edition and answer key
builds only (`audience="teacher"`).

::: exercise {#exr-one-step difficulty=1}
Solve $x + 9 = 4$.

::: answer {audience="teacher"}
$x = -5$.
:::
:::

::: exercise {#exr-two-step difficulty=1}
Solve $5x - 2 = 13$.

::: answer {audience="teacher"}
$x = 3$.
:::
:::

::: exercise {#exr-fractions difficulty=2}
Solve $\dfrac{x}{4} + 7 = 12$.

::: hint
Undo the $+7$ first, then the division by 4.
:::

::: answer {audience="teacher"}
$x = 20$.
:::
:::

::: exercise {#exr-both-sides difficulty=2 standards="CCSS.MATH.8.EE.C.7.b"}
Solve $9x + 1 = 4x + 21$.

::: answer {audience="teacher"}
$x = 4$.
:::
:::

::: exercise {#exr-distribute difficulty=2}
Solve $3(2x - 5) = 21$.

::: answer {audience="teacher"}
$x = 6$. Accept either distributing first or dividing both sides by 3
first — the second is slicker; ask who found it.
:::
:::

::: exercise {#exr-classify difficulty=3 standards="CCSS.MATH.8.EE.C.7.a"}
Without fully solving, decide whether each equation has one solution, no
solution, or infinitely many. Explain how you know.

1. $4(x + 2) = 4x + 8$
2. $4(x + 2) = 4x + 10$
3. $4(x + 2) = 3x + 8$

::: answer {audience="teacher"}
1. Infinitely many — both sides distribute to the identical expression.
2. No solution — the variable terms cancel, leaving $8 = 10$, false.
3. One solution ($x = 0$) — the variable terms don't cancel.

Watch for students who answer (3) "no solution" because the solution is
zero; it's the most common conflation in this cluster.
:::
:::

::: exercise {#exr-write-own difficulty=3}
Write a linear equation with variables on both sides whose solution is
$x = -2$. Trade with a partner and solve each other's equations.

::: answer {audience="teacher"}
Answers vary; verify by substitution. A quick construction to share:
start from $x = -2$ and apply the same operations to both sides in
forward order, e.g. multiply by 3 and add $x + 5$ to both sides.
:::
:::
